   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.3",
    "topic_title": "Elemental Composition of Pure Substances",
    "stem": "A 92.0 g sample of a pure compound containing only nitrogen and oxygen contains 28.0 g of nitrogen and 64.0 g of oxygen. The molar masses of nitrogen and oxygen atoms are 14.0 g/mol and 16.0 g/mol, respectively. What is the empirical formula of the compound?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "N₇O₁₆",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Mass-ratio trap: Reducing 28:64 gives 7:16, but formula subscripts represent atom ratios, not mass ratios. Nitrogen and oxygen have different atomic molar masses. Fix: Convert each element’s mass to moles before reducing the ratio."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "N₂O₄",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Unreduced-ratio trap: The calculated mole amounts give N:O = 2:4, but an empirical formula requires the lowest whole-number ratio. Both values share a factor of 2. Fix: Divide every mole amount by the smallest before writing subscripts."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "NO₂",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The sample contains 28.0/14.0 = 2.00 mol of nitrogen atoms and 64.0/16.0 = 4.00 mol of oxygen atoms. Dividing both amounts by 2.00 gives the lowest whole-number atom ratio, N:O = 1:2, so the empirical formula is NO₂."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "N₂O",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-subscript trap: This formula assigns twice as many atoms to nitrogen as to oxygen. The calculated amounts are 2.00 mol N and 4.00 mol O, so oxygen must have the larger subscript. Fix: Label each mole amount with its element before assigning subscripts."
     }
    ]
   },
